var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:14px}.q-text{font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px;font-size:.92em}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;transition:all .15s;color:var(--text-sec)}.q-opt:hover{border-color:var(--purple);background:rgba(167,139,250,.06)}.q-opt.correct{border-color:var(--emerald)!important;background:rgba(52,211,153,.1)!important;color:var(--emerald)!important}.q-opt.wrong{border-color:var(--red)!important;background:rgba(239,68,68,.1)!important;color:var(--red)!important}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none;opacity:.7}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:10px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-soln.show{display:block}.nav-links a:hover{color:var(--text)}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span><div class="nav-links"><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a><a href="chemistry.html">Chemistry</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Electrochemistry — Galvanic Cells, Nernst Equation, Electrolysis &amp; Conductance</h2><p>Deep dive into Electrochemistry — the study of chemical processes that involve the transfer of electrons, converting chemical energy to electrical energy (galvanic cells) and using electrical energy to drive non-spontaneous reactions (electrolytic cells). Galvanic (voltaic) cells: the anode (where oxidation occurs, negative sign for galvanic cells) and the cathode (where reduction occurs, positive sign). Cell notation: Zn(s) | Zn²?(aq) || Cu²?(aq) | Cu(s). The standard cell potential E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode (using standard reduction potentials). The standard hydrogen electrode (SHE): Pt | H2(1 bar) | H?(1 M), assigned E° = 0.00 V. The electrochemical series ranks reduction potentials; a more positive E°_red means a stronger oxidizing agent. The Nernst equation: E = E° - (RT/nF) ln Q = E° - (0.05916 V/n) log Q at 25°C. The relationship between cell potential and thermodynamics: ?G = -nFE (?G° = -nFE°), and at equilibrium, E = 0 so ln K = nFE°/(RT) = nE°/0.0257 at 25°C, or log K = nE°/0.05916. Concentration cells produce a potential from a difference in concentration: E = -(RT/nF) ln([dilute]/[concentrated]). Electrolysis: using an external voltage to drive a non-spontaneous reaction. Faraday&apos;s laws: (1) the mass of a substance produced at an electrode is proportional to the quantity of electricity passed; (2) m = (Q/F)(M/z), where Q = I × t (coulombs), F = 96,485 C/mol (Faraday constant), M is molar mass, z is the number of electrons per ion. Electroplating, electrolysis of water (2H2O ? 2H2 + O2, requiring a minimum of 1.23 V but typically more due to overpotential). Conductance: specific conductivity ? (S/cm), molar conductivity ?_m = ?/c (S·cm²/mol). Kohlrausch&apos;s law: ?_m = ?_m° - Kvc for strong electrolytes (linear at low concentrations). For weak electrolytes, ?_m increases sharply at low concentration as dissociation increases. ?_m° (limiting molar conductivity) is the sum of the ionic contributions: ?_m° = ?_+ ?_+° + ?_- ?_-° (Kohlrausch&apos;s law of independent migration). Applications: determining K_a of a weak acid from conductivity measurements, conductometric titrations.</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">&quot;Electrochemistry is the bridge between chemistry and electricity — the study of the interplay between chemical change and electrical energy.&quot;</div><div class="attribution">— Adapted from Arrhenius and Nernst</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>?? Galvanic Cells and Standard Electrode Potentials</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Galvanic Cells</div><div class="story-title">Cell Construction, Standard Reduction Potentials, and the Electrochemical Series</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>galvanic (voltaic) cell</strong> produces electrical energy from a spontaneous redox reaction. The cell consists of two half-cells connected by a salt bridge (which maintains electrical neutrality by allowing ions to flow). The <strong>anode</strong> is where oxidation occurs (Zn ? Zn²? + 2e?); in a galvanic cell the anode is negative (electrons flow from it). The <strong>cathode</strong> is where reduction occurs (Cu²? + 2e? ? Cu); the cathode is positive. <strong>Cell notation</strong>: anode | anode solution || cathode solution | cathode. The single vertical line represents a phase boundary, the double line represents the salt bridge. The <strong>standard cell potential</strong> E°_cell = E°_cathode(reduction) - E°_anode(reduction) = E°_red(cathode) - E°_red(anode). Standard reduction potentials are measured relative to the <strong>standard hydrogen electrode (SHE)</strong>: Pt(s) | H2(g, 1 bar) | H?(aq, 1 M), assigned E° = 0.000 V by convention. The <strong>electrochemical series</strong> lists half-reactions in order of decreasing reduction potential. The most positive reduction potentials (F2 + 2e? ? 2F?, E° = +2.87 V) correspond to the strongest oxidizing agents. The most negative (Li? + e? ? Li, E° = -3.04 V) correspond to the strongest reducing agents. E°_cell must be positive for a spontaneous galvanic cell (?G° = -nFE°_cell < 0). The cell potential depends on temperature, pressure, and concentration via the Nernst equation. Batteries are practical galvanic cells: the Daniell cell (Zn-Cu), the lead-acid battery (Pb + PbO2 + 2H2SO4 ? 2PbSO4 + 2H2O, E° ˜ 2.05 V), alkaline batteries (Zn-MnO2), lithium-ion batteries (LiCoO2/graphite, E° ˜ 3.7 V), and fuel cells (H2 + ½O2 ? H2O, E° = 1.23 V).</div><div class="story-moral">A galvanic cell generates electricity from spontaneous redox reactions; the cell potential is determined by the difference in reduction potentials of the two half-cells against the standard hydrogen electrode.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>?? The Nernst Equation and Thermodynamic Connections</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Nernst Equation</div><div class="story-title">E = E° - (RT/nF) ln Q, Concentration Cells, and the Relation to ?G and K</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Nernst equation</strong> relates the cell potential to the concentrations of species: E = E° - (RT/nF) ln Q. At 25°C, RT/F = 0.025693 V, and converting ln to log10 gives E = E° - (0.05916 V/n) log Q. For the Daniell cell (Zn|Zn²? || Cu²?|Cu), E = E° - (0.05916/2) log([Zn²?]/[Cu²?]). <strong>Concentration cells</strong> have identical half-cells but different concentrations: E = -(RT/nF) ln([dilute]/[concentrated]) — the cell produces a potential until concentrations equalize. The thermodynamic connection: ?G = -nFE. Under standard conditions, ?G° = -nFE°. Since ?G° = -RT ln K, we get ln K = nFE°/(RT). At 25°C, log K = nE°/0.05916. This allows calculation of equilibrium constants from measured cell potentials. <strong>pH measurement</strong>: a glass electrode measures the potential difference across a thin glass membrane that is sensitive to H? concentration; the pH meter reads E = constant + (RT/F) ln[H?] = constant - (0.05916 V) × pH. The Nernst equation also applies to ion-selective electrodes (ISEs) for measuring specific ion concentrations. For the half-reaction O2 + 4H? + 4e? ? 2H2O (E° = 1.229 V), the potential depends on pH: E = 1.229 - (0.05916)pH at 1 atm O2. <strong>Fuel cells</strong> use the reverse of electrolysis: H2 and O2 combine to produce electricity directly, with E = 1.23 V under standard conditions but operating at higher temperatures (up to 1000°C in solid oxide fuel cells) to improve efficiency.</div><div class="story-moral">The Nernst equation connects the cell potential to concentration via the reaction quotient; its thermodynamic foundation links electrochemistry to ?G and equilibrium constants.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>? Electrolysis and Faraday&apos;s Laws</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Electrolysis</div><div class="story-title">Faraday&apos;s Laws, Overpotential, Electroplating, and the Electrolysis of Water</div><div class="story-text">An <strong>electrolytic cell</strong> uses electrical energy to drive a non-spontaneous redox reaction. The external power supply forces electrons to flow from the anode (now positive, where oxidation occurs) to the cathode (now negative, where reduction occurs) — opposite to a galvanic cell. <strong>Faraday&apos;s first law</strong>: the mass of a substance produced at an electrode is directly proportional to the quantity of electricity (Q) passed through the cell. <strong>Faraday&apos;s second law</strong>: for a given quantity of electricity, the mass of a substance produced is proportional to its equivalent weight (M/z). Combined: m = (Q/F)(M/z) = (I t M)/(z F), where I is current (amperes), t is time (seconds), F = 96,485 C/mol, M is molar mass, z is the number of electrons transferred per ion. Example: to plate 1.00 g of Cu from Cu²? solution, Q = m z F / M = (1.00 × 2 × 96485)/(63.55) = 3037 C. At 1.00 A, this requires 3037 s ˜ 50.6 min. <strong>Overpotential</strong> (?): the extra voltage required above the thermodynamic E°_cell to overcome kinetic barriers (activation overpotential) and concentration gradients (concentration overpotential). For water electrolysis, the thermodynamic potential is 1.23 V, but in practice about 1.8-2.0 V is needed due to overpotential. The electrolysis of water: 2H2O(l) ? 2H2(g) + O2(g). At the cathode: 2H2O + 2e? ? H2 + 2OH?; at the anode: 2H2O ? O2 + 4H? + 4e?. The ratio of gas volumes is 2:1 (H2:O2). <strong>Electroplating</strong> uses electrolysis to deposit a thin metal coating (e.g., Ag, Au, Cr, Ni) onto a conductive surface. The object to be plated is the cathode; the anode is the plating metal. <strong>Electrorefining</strong> purifies metals (e.g., copper): impure copper is the anode, pure copper deposits at the cathode; impurities fall to the bottom as anode sludge (containing Ag, Au, Pt). <strong>Electrochemical corrosion</strong>: the rusting of iron involves anodic oxidation (Fe ? Fe²? + 2e?) and cathodic reduction (O2 + 4H? + 4e? ? 2H2O, or O2 + 2H2O + 4e? ? 4OH? in neutral conditions). Cathodic protection (e.g., using a sacrificial zinc anode) prevents corrosion by making the iron the cathode.</div><div class="story-moral">Electrolysis uses electrical energy to drive non-spontaneous reactions; Faraday&apos;s laws quantify the relationship between charge passed and the mass of product formed.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>?? Conductance and Ionic Mobility</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Conductance</div><div class="story-title">Specific Conductivity, Molar Conductivity, Kohlrausch&apos;s Law, and Conductometric Titrations</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Specific conductivity</strong> ? (S/cm) is the conductance of a 1 cm cube of solution: ? = G × (cell constant), where G = 1/R (siemens) is the conductance and the cell constant = l/A (electrode separation/area). <strong>Molar conductivity</strong> ?_m = ?/c (S·cm²/mol), where c is concentration (mol/cm³). As concentration decreases, ?_m increases because interionic attractions weaken (for strong electrolytes) or dissociation increases (for weak electrolytes). <strong>Kohlrausch&apos;s law</strong>: for strong electrolytes at low concentrations, ?_m = ?_m° - Kvc, where K is a constant depending on the electrolyte type and ?_m° is the <strong>limiting molar conductivity</strong> (at infinite dilution). <strong>Kohlrausch&apos;s law of independent migration of ions</strong>: ?_m° = ?_+ ?_+° + ?_- ?_-°, where ?_+ and ?_- are the number of cations and anions per formula unit, and ?_+°, ?_-° are the limiting ionic conductivities. For example, ?_m°(NaCl) = ?°(Na?) + ?°(Cl?) = 50.1 + 76.3 = 126.4 S·cm²/mol. For weak electrolytes, ?_m° can be found by combining strong electrolyte values: ?_m°(CH3COOH) = ?_m°(HCl) + ?_m°(CH3COONa) - ?_m°(NaCl). Once ?_m° is known, the degree of dissociation a = ?_m/?_m° for a weak electrolyte, and K_a = (c a²)/(1 - a) for a 1:1 weak acid. <strong>Conductometric titrations</strong> monitor conductivity changes during a titration. For strong acid + strong base: conductivity drops to a minimum at the equivalence point (H? replaced by less mobile Na?), then rises due to excess OH?. For weak acid + strong base: conductivity starts low, increases slightly as the salt forms, then rises sharply after equivalence. <strong>Ionic mobility</strong> u = v_drift/E (cm²/V·s) is related to ?° by ?° = z F u. Ions with smaller hydrated radii (H?, OH?) have anomalously high mobilities due to the Grotthuss mechanism (proton hopping along hydrogen-bonded water chains). The <strong>transport number</strong> t_+ = ?_+/?_m = u_+/(u_+ + u_-) is the fraction of current carried by the cation.</div><div class="story-moral">Conductance measurements reveal the extent of electrolyte dissociation; Kohlrausch&apos;s law of independent ionic migration allows the determination of limiting molar conductivities and acid dissociation constants for weak electrolytes.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">?? Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">?? Reset All</button><a href="chemistry.html" class="btn btn-success">? Back to Chemistry</a></div></div></div><script>
